# AthletOS scheduled work.
#
# Installed by infra/bootstrap.sh to /usr/local/etc/cron.d/athletos. FreeBSD's
# cron has read /etc/cron.d and /usr/local/etc/cron.d since 13.0; on anything
# older, paste these four lines into /etc/crontab instead — the format is the
# same, six fields with a user column.
#
# Nothing here is in periodic(8). periodic is for the base system's own daily
# and weekly housekeeping, and putting application jobs in it means their output
# arrives inside the daily security run where nobody reads it.

SHELL=/bin/sh
PATH=/sbin:/bin:/usr/sbin:/usr/bin:/usr/local/sbin:/usr/local/bin
MAILTO=root

# Snapshots. sanoid is a no-op when nothing is due, so running it every minute
# is both the documented usage and cheaper than reasoning about which minute the
# 15-minute snapshots should land on.
*       *       *       *       *       root    /usr/local/bin/sanoid --cron --quiet

# The backup. Five past the hour rather than on the hour, so it is not competing
# with sanoid's hourly snapshot for the same page cache.
5       *       *       *       *       root    /usr/local/sbin/athletos-backup

# A backup that has not been restored is a rumour. This does not restore
# anything — it checks that the newest offsite dump is recent and is not
# suspiciously small, which catches the two failures that actually happen: the
# push silently stopping, and pg_dump writing nothing. The real drill is
# `athletos-restore --drill`, which needs the age identity and therefore needs a
# human; put it in the calendar quarterly.
17      6       *       *       *       root    /usr/local/sbin/athletos-backup --check-only

# Certificates renew themselves — that is the entire reason Caddy is here rather
# than nginx and certbot — so there is no renewal entry, no reload hook, and no
# expired certificate on a Sunday.
